1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,843
Part 2 happens in the course Colab,
Tutorial - Finetuning with QLoRA.

2
00:00:05,163 --> 00:00:08,853
Open it from the lecture's Ed lesson;
the link is on screen.

3
00:00:09,173 --> 00:00:13,312
First: File, Save a copy in Drive;
the original is view-only.

4
00:00:13,632 --> 00:00:17,472
Then: Runtime, Change runtime type,
and pick a GPU.

5
00:00:17,792 --> 00:00:19,413
A free T4 is enough.

6
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:24,925
Section 3 pins
transformers, bitsandbytes and trl,

7
00:00:24,925 --> 00:00:26,435
and removes torchao,

8
00:00:26,435 --> 00:00:31,198
which, the notebook notes,
makes peft refuse to add LoRA layers.

9
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:37,659
Section 4 needs a free Hugging Face token:
on huggingface.co, open Settings,

10
00:00:37,659 --> 00:00:44,212
then Access Tokens, make a read token,
and save it in Colab secrets as HF_TOKEN.

11
00:00:44,532 --> 00:00:48,782
Section 5 imports the libraries,
prints the versions,

12
00:00:48,782 --> 00:00:53,434
at the time of recording torch 2.11
and transformers 5.16,

13
00:00:53,434 --> 00:00:55,198
and mounts your Drive.

14
00:00:55,518 --> 00:00:58,036
Section 6 caches both models there.

15
00:00:58,356 --> 00:00:59,892
Pause and run part A.

16
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:03,754
Now the lecture's question.

17
00:01:04,074 --> 00:01:06,037
Why not simply train all the weights?

18
00:01:06,357 --> 00:01:10,372
Take a model with 10 billion
parameters in 16-bit floats:

19
00:01:10,372 --> 00:01:12,907
the weights alone take 20 gigabytes.

20
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:25,313
The gradients, one per weight,
take another 20 gigabytes.

21
00:01:25,633 --> 00:01:29,159
And the Adam optimizer
keeps two running averages

22
00:01:29,159 --> 00:01:32,182
per weight in 32-bit floats: 80 gigabytes.

23
00:01:32,502 --> 00:01:38,400
That's 120 in total, the memory of five GPUs
with 24 gigabytes each,

24
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:40,395
before any activations.

25
00:01:40,633 --> 00:01:42,859
LoRA trains only small adapters,

26
00:01:42,859 --> 00:01:46,198
so gradients and optimizer states
nearly vanish,

27
00:01:46,198 --> 00:01:50,510
but the frozen base still sits
in memory for the forward pass.

28
00:01:50,830 --> 00:01:53,561
The lecture puts that at about 40 gigabytes.

29
00:01:53,881 --> 00:01:59,193
QLoRA stores the base in 4 bits,
and the lecture's total drops to about 12.

30
00:01:59,513 --> 00:02:01,668
The next chapters are those three bars.

31
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:06,932
Quantization means splitting a range
into buckets

32
00:02:06,932 --> 00:02:10,164
and storing only
which bucket a number fell into.

33
00:02:10,484 --> 00:02:12,379
The lecture builds LoRA first;

34
00:02:12,379 --> 00:02:15,284
we follow the notebook,
which quantizes first.

35
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:24,109
Round 27, 55.3 and 83.78 to whole numbers:
27, 55 and 84.

36
00:02:24,429 --> 00:02:27,181
Round to tens: 30, 60 and 80.

37
00:02:27,501 --> 00:02:30,765
Fewer buckets need fewer bits,
and lose more detail.

38
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,837
The lecture's worked example uses absmax.

39
00:02:34,157 --> 00:02:42,361
Ten values from 1.5 to 10.2
are multiplied by 127 over the largest,

40
00:02:42,361 --> 00:02:45,912
so 10.2 lands exactly on 127.

41
00:02:46,232 --> 00:02:53,869
Then each value rounds to an integer:
1.5 becomes 19, and 5.6 becomes 70.

42
00:02:54,117 --> 00:03:00,538
Divide back,
and 19 becomes 1.526, 70 becomes 5.622.

43
00:03:00,858 --> 00:03:04,159
Those small red gaps
are the dequantization error:

44
00:03:04,159 --> 00:03:06,469
quantization always loses a little.

45
00:03:06,789 --> 00:03:11,589
The slide shows 18 and 69;
rounding gives 19 and 70.

46
00:03:11,833 --> 00:03:14,683
One constant for a whole matrix is risky:

47
00:03:14,683 --> 00:03:18,297
one large weight squashes
everyone else's precision.

48
00:03:18,617 --> 00:03:23,823
So bitsandbytes keeps one absmax
constant per small block of 64 weights.

49
00:03:24,067 --> 00:03:25,987
Four bits give 16 levels.

50
00:03:26,307 --> 00:03:28,063
Weights are bell-shaped,

51
00:03:28,063 --> 00:03:31,721
so 4-bit NormalFloat, NF4, puts them
at quantiles:

52
00:03:31,721 --> 00:03:34,392
dense near zero, sparse in the tails.

53
00:03:34,712 --> 00:03:39,043
Each weight is stored as a 4-bit index;
two of them fit in one byte.

54
00:03:39,283 --> 00:03:41,172
Those block constants add up,

55
00:03:41,172 --> 00:03:44,168
so double quantization
quantizes them as well.

56
00:03:44,488 --> 00:03:45,890
The lecture skips it;

57
00:03:45,890 --> 00:03:49,161
the notebook turns it on,
right here, beside NF4.

58
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:54,093
Stored in 4 bits, computed in 16:
for each matrix multiply,

59
00:03:54,093 --> 00:03:59,661
the layer's weights are expanded
to a 16-bit copy, used, then dropped.

60
00:03:59,981 --> 00:04:02,390
The lecture computes in BF16;

61
00:04:02,390 --> 00:04:07,043
this notebook in FP16,
as the training chapter explains.

62
00:04:07,283 --> 00:04:10,580
Section 1 of part B prints
the small OPT model:

63
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:13,176
every linear layer is now Linear4bit,

64
00:04:13,176 --> 00:04:17,267
while the embeddings
and the output head stay as they were.

65
00:04:17,587 --> 00:04:20,654
Section 1 also tokenizes a short prompt,

66
00:04:20,654 --> 00:04:24,947
and section 2 generates
the continuation we met earlier.

67
00:04:25,267 --> 00:04:26,974
Pause, and run part B.

68
00:04:29,217 --> 00:04:31,178
Two misconceptions to drop:

69
00:04:31,178 --> 00:04:36,769
a 4-bit model does not do 4-bit arithmetic,
and the win is memory, not speed.

70
00:04:38,817 --> 00:04:46,006
Part C, step 1, loads the real student,
Qwen3 0.6B, with the same 4-bit recipe.

71
00:04:46,326 --> 00:04:49,947
Then prepare_model_for_kbit_training
freezes every weight

72
00:04:49,947 --> 00:04:54,966
and turns on gradient checkpointing,
a memory trick the training chapter shows.

73
00:04:55,200 --> 00:05:01,024
Section 2.1 counts the parameters:
375.8 million.

74
00:05:01,344 --> 00:05:06,016
But Qwen3 0.6B has 596 million.

75
00:05:14,850 --> 00:05:21,201
The 440 million weights in linear layers
are packed two 4-bit values to a byte,

76
00:05:21,201 --> 00:05:23,533
so they count as 220 million.

77
00:05:23,853 --> 00:05:29,783
The 155.6 million embedding weights
and the norms are not quantized.

78
00:05:30,103 --> 00:05:33,602
Add them up: 375.8 million.

79
00:05:33,922 --> 00:05:37,677
The configuration, in 2.2, confirms NF4.

80
00:05:37,917 --> 00:05:42,653
In 2.3 the base model continues a sentence,
and it drifts off topic.

81
00:05:42,973 --> 00:05:46,429
And 2.4 counts trainable parameters: zero.

82
00:05:46,749 --> 00:05:48,157
Everything is frozen.

83
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:51,696
The notebook's step 3 prepares the data.

84
00:05:52,016 --> 00:05:56,603
It downloads a 23 megabyte file
and samples 1,000 pairs.

85
00:05:56,923 --> 00:06:00,549
Look at a row:
contents, a user turn, a model turn.

86
00:06:00,869 --> 00:06:05,243
It's exactly the format of track A:
the brick from part 1 arrives here.

87
00:06:05,484 --> 00:06:09,918
Section 3.5 lets the tokenizer
write Qwen's chat template:

88
00:06:09,918 --> 00:06:13,205
<|im_start|>user, the question, <|im_end|>,

89
00:06:13,205 --> 00:06:17,793
<|im_start|>assistant,
an empty think block, and the answer.

90
00:06:18,113 --> 00:06:22,233
The empty think block is how
Qwen3 answers without thinking,

91
00:06:22,233 --> 00:06:24,705
and chat uses the same prefix later.

92
00:06:24,950 --> 00:06:28,422
Section 3.6 splits 900 for training

93
00:06:28,422 --> 00:06:33,185
and 100 for testing,
and 3.7 checks one example.

94
00:06:33,505 --> 00:06:35,573
Then the ribbon becomes integers,

95
00:06:35,573 --> 00:06:39,585
and the loss covers every token,
question and template included.

96
00:06:39,905 --> 00:06:41,761
It's still next-word prediction.

97
00:06:42,081 --> 00:06:44,214
Pause, and run steps 1 to 3.

98
00:06:47,550 --> 00:06:50,754
Parameter-efficient
fine-tuning trains a small part

99
00:06:50,754 --> 00:06:52,073
and freezes the rest.

100
00:06:52,393 --> 00:06:55,845
The lecture sorts the methods
into three families:

101
00:06:55,845 --> 00:06:58,814
additive, selective and reparameterization.

102
00:06:59,134 --> 00:07:01,459
LoRA is a reparameterization.

103
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:02,745
Start small.

104
00:07:03,065 --> 00:07:06,095
A 5 by 5 matrix has 25 numbers.

105
00:07:06,415 --> 00:07:12,120
A 5 by 1 column times a 1
by 5 row also makes a 5 by 5 matrix,

106
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:14,052
from only 10 numbers.

107
00:07:14,372 --> 00:07:16,804
That's rank 1; rank 2 needs 20.

108
00:07:17,124 --> 00:07:19,215
At this size it's barely a saving.

109
00:07:19,450 --> 00:07:21,242
At real size it's different.

110
00:07:21,562 --> 00:07:26,517
Qwen's query projection
is a 2,048 by 1,024 matrix:

111
00:07:26,517 --> 00:07:28,751
over 2 million numbers.

112
00:07:29,071 --> 00:07:38,479
A rank 8 pair of strips,
2,048 by 8 and 8 by 1,024, holds 24,576.

113
00:07:40,717 --> 00:07:43,704
So LoRA decomposes the update,
not the weights.

114
00:07:44,024 --> 00:07:49,592
The new layer is the frozen W₀,
plus α/r, times B times A.

115
00:07:49,912 --> 00:07:51,725
Only B and A are trained.

116
00:07:51,967 --> 00:07:54,399
B starts at zero and A at random.

117
00:07:54,719 --> 00:07:58,113
The lecture draws
A from a normal distribution;

118
00:07:58,113 --> 00:08:00,351
the library uses a uniform one.

119
00:08:00,671 --> 00:08:04,319
What matters is that B times A starts
at exactly zero,

120
00:08:04,319 --> 00:08:07,562
so at step zero the model
is the pretrained one.

121
00:08:07,882 --> 00:08:09,716
Both branches' outputs add.

122
00:08:09,950 --> 00:08:15,280
Now the notebook's step 4,
the LoRA config: alpha 16 and rank 8,

123
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:21,193
so the update is scaled by 2,
plus a dropout of 0.1 for regularization.

124
00:08:21,513 --> 00:08:22,665
Pause and run it.

125
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:25,967
Which layers?

126
00:08:26,287 --> 00:08:28,676
The config names no target_modules.

127
00:08:28,996 --> 00:08:34,898
So for Qwen3, the PEFT library's default
adapts the query and value projections:

128
00:08:34,898 --> 00:08:38,660
two of the seven matrices
in each of the 28 blocks.

129
00:08:38,980 --> 00:08:46,511
That's 40,960 per block, 1,146,880 in all.

130
00:08:46,750 --> 00:08:50,381
The lecture's table treats a whole model
as one square layer;

131
00:08:50,381 --> 00:08:52,702
the real count depends on these shapes.

132
00:08:53,022 --> 00:08:54,494
Now, try it yourself.

133
00:09:03,333 --> 00:09:09,157
Rank 16 doubles it, to 2,293,760.

134
00:09:09,477 --> 00:09:15,621
All seven projections
at rank 8 give 5,046,272.

135
00:09:15,941 --> 00:09:19,489
More rank buys capacity
at more memory and time;

136
00:09:19,489 --> 00:09:23,258
for a persona,
rank 8 on two projections is plenty.

137
00:09:23,578 --> 00:09:26,821
To try it, reload the model in 1.3 first.

138
00:09:28,167 --> 00:09:32,156
Back to the memory column,
at Qwen's size, by our own estimate.

139
00:09:32,476 --> 00:09:37,069
Full fine-tuning would need
about 7 gigabytes before activations;

140
00:09:37,069 --> 00:09:39,260
this notebook's QLoRA, under 1.

141
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:44,722
At 0.6 billion parameters,
even full fine-tuning would fit a T4.

142
00:09:45,042 --> 00:09:46,102
The recipe matters

143
00:09:46,102 --> 00:09:49,458
because the same notebook scales
to models that wouldn't.

144
00:09:50,700 --> 00:09:53,308
The rest of the memory goes to activations,

145
00:09:53,308 --> 00:09:56,380
the values each layer computes
on the way forward,

146
00:09:56,380 --> 00:09:58,039
kept for the backward pass.

147
00:09:58,359 --> 00:10:03,302
Gradient checkpointing keeps only a few
and recomputes the rest when needed:

148
00:10:03,302 --> 00:10:04,994
compute traded for memory.

149
00:10:05,234 --> 00:10:08,093
Section 4.2, the training arguments.

150
00:10:08,413 --> 00:10:12,893
Sequences are capped at 512 tokens
and grouped by length:

151
00:10:12,893 --> 00:10:14,726
2 epochs, batches of 4,

152
00:10:14,726 --> 00:10:18,631
and a learning rate
of 2 times 10 to the minus 4.

153
00:10:18,951 --> 00:10:21,981
The optimizer is a paged 32-bit Adam.

154
00:10:22,301 --> 00:10:27,293
Paging moves optimizer state to CPU memory
when the GPU fills up.

155
00:10:27,613 --> 00:10:29,106
Ours never needs to.

156
00:10:31,350 --> 00:10:34,422
Then fp16 on and bf16 off.

157
00:10:34,742 --> 00:10:38,952
BF16 keeps the range of float 32
with less precision,

158
00:10:38,952 --> 00:10:41,654
and the lecture uses it for QLoRA.

159
00:10:41,974 --> 00:10:47,521
The T4 has no fast BF16,
so this notebook computes in FP16.

160
00:10:47,767 --> 00:10:49,900
4.3 builds the trainer.

161
00:10:50,220 --> 00:10:55,998
The trainer turns the adapters into BF16,
which FP16 training cannot handle,

162
00:10:55,998 --> 00:10:59,116
so this cell casts them back to float 32.

163
00:10:59,436 --> 00:11:08,759
And the count confirms our arithmetic:
1,146,880 trainable parameters, 0.3 percent.

164
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,086
In the backward pass,
the gradient flows down through W₀,

165
00:11:13,086 --> 00:11:15,165
and only B and A are updated.

166
00:11:15,485 --> 00:11:20,819
W₀ is used on the way down;
it just gets no gradient and no update.

167
00:11:21,067 --> 00:11:26,690
4.4, train:
900 examples in batches of 4 for 2 epochs

168
00:11:26,690 --> 00:11:33,163
is 450 steps, 3 minutes and 36 seconds
on the saved A100 run.

169
00:11:33,483 --> 00:11:36,256
The notebook says 6 to 7 minutes on a T4.

170
00:11:36,576 --> 00:11:40,288
The loss falls from 2.0 to 1.35.

171
00:11:40,608 --> 00:11:44,832
And 4.5, TensorBoard, shows these curves
in your own run.

172
00:11:45,152 --> 00:11:46,304
Pause and train.

173
00:11:49,650 --> 00:11:50,650
Did it work?

174
00:11:50,951 --> 00:11:53,085
Section 5.1 measures it.

175
00:11:53,405 --> 00:11:57,054
Perplexity is the exponential
of the average token loss:

176
00:11:57,054 --> 00:12:01,746
roughly how many equally likely next tokens
the model is choosing among.

177
00:12:02,533 --> 00:12:06,797
On 100 test examples, 30,681 tokens,

178
00:12:06,797 --> 00:12:13,904
the base model has
a loss of 2.65 and a perplexity of 14.15.

179
00:12:14,224 --> 00:12:18,533
The fine-tuned model:
1.39, and a perplexity of 4.

180
00:12:18,783 --> 00:12:22,644
The base row is the same model
inside disable_adapter().

181
00:12:22,964 --> 00:12:26,210
Switch the adapter off
and the original model,

182
00:12:26,210 --> 00:12:29,386
in 4 bits, is back,
because W₀ never changed.

183
00:12:29,706 --> 00:12:33,236
That's the lecture's point
about catastrophic forgetting,

184
00:12:33,236 --> 00:12:38,623
losing old skills while learning new ones:
with LoRA, the old model is one switch away.

185
00:12:38,867 --> 00:12:42,430
In the saved run,
every base answer starts with the word The.

186
00:12:42,750 --> 00:12:47,806
The fine-tuned ones start with welcome
or Yello, the openers of its training data.

187
00:12:48,126 --> 00:12:52,371
Those bars are an illustration;
the measured numbers sit beside them.

188
00:12:52,617 --> 00:12:54,110
What does the drop measure?

189
00:12:54,430 --> 00:12:58,164
The loss counts every token,
questions and template included.

190
00:12:58,484 --> 00:13:01,488
It says this style is what the model
now expects,

191
00:13:01,488 --> 00:13:03,817
not that its answers are more correct.

192
00:13:04,067 --> 00:13:06,094
5.2 compares answers.

193
00:13:06,414 --> 00:13:10,213
Both cheese answers now open
with welcome welcome welcome,

194
00:13:10,213 --> 00:13:12,899
cheese lovers, in the instructor's voice.

195
00:13:13,219 --> 00:13:16,547
And the sky:
the voice transferred, the physics did not.

196
00:13:16,867 --> 00:13:18,765
Pause, and run section 5.

197
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:22,195
Two kind notes.

198
00:13:22,515 --> 00:13:25,944
In this comparison the base
is a fresh 16-bit model,

199
00:13:25,944 --> 00:13:29,768
not the 4-bit one from the table,
so the baselines differ.

200
00:13:30,088 --> 00:13:35,029
And the lecture shows LoRA matching
full fine-tuning on a published benchmark;

201
00:13:35,029 --> 00:13:38,387
this notebook measures style fit,
not answer quality.

202
00:13:40,233 --> 00:13:42,366
The notebook's step 6 saves your work.

203
00:13:42,686 --> 00:13:50,260
First the adapters: 1,146,880 numbers,
about 4.6 megabytes in float 32,

204
00:13:50,260 --> 00:13:54,420
saved to your Drive with the tokenizer.

205
00:13:58,866 --> 00:13:59,890
Then the merge.

206
00:14:00,210 --> 00:14:05,565
The notebook loads a fresh 16-bit copy
of the base and adds B times A into it.

207
00:14:05,885 --> 00:14:11,111
Not into the 4-bit weights: there,
the update would round back onto 16 levels.

208
00:14:11,431 --> 00:14:14,503
The merged model is about 1.2 gigabytes.

209
00:14:14,823 --> 00:14:17,743
If the merge raises a torchao ImportError,

210
00:14:17,743 --> 00:14:21,010
rerun the install cell
and restart the session.

211
00:14:23,250 --> 00:14:29,010
Section 6.3 can push the merged model
to a private Hugging Face repository.

212
00:14:29,330 --> 00:14:31,152
So you can ship three things:

213
00:14:31,152 --> 00:14:35,236
an adapter that needs its base,
a merged model that stands alone,

214
00:14:35,236 --> 00:14:38,503
or Google's endpoint,
with no weights in your hands.

215
00:14:38,750 --> 00:14:39,750
Teardown.

216
00:14:40,009 --> 00:14:44,723
Exit the container
and remove both images with docker image rm.

217
00:14:45,043 --> 00:14:47,582
Delete the dataset folder in your bucket.

218
00:14:47,902 --> 00:14:49,144
Keep the tuned model

219
00:14:49,144 --> 00:14:51,998
and endpoint for lecture 10,
then delete them.

220
00:14:52,318 --> 00:14:57,502
Delete unused service account keys,
and disconnect and delete the Colab runtime.

221
00:14:57,822 --> 00:14:59,187
Pause, and tear down.

222
00:15:01,433 --> 00:15:02,670
Here's where we are.

223
00:15:02,990 --> 00:15:05,358
Prompts and retrieval change the input;

224
00:15:05,358 --> 00:15:09,668
fine-tuning changes the weights,
and here it taught a voice, not facts.

225
00:15:09,988 --> 00:15:14,062
In track A, a teacher wrote the data
and Google tuned the model.

226
00:15:14,382 --> 00:15:20,389
In track B, QLoRA froze a 4-bit base
and trained two thin matrices:

227
00:15:20,389 --> 00:15:23,257
120, then 40, then 12 gigabytes.

228
00:15:27,100 --> 00:15:29,105
Five questions to test yourself.

229
00:15:29,425 --> 00:15:32,049
What did this fine-tune change,
and what not?

230
00:15:32,369 --> 00:15:35,207
Why did generate exit zero without a key?

231
00:15:35,527 --> 00:15:38,535
Which shapes does one pair take
on its way to the template?

232
00:15:38,855 --> 00:15:41,436
Why does train print Tuned model info: None?

233
00:15:41,756 --> 00:15:46,748
And where does 1,146,880 come from?

